sections.s2_technical:
The reason you cannot simply change the event is coupling in time. A synchronous API change can be negotiated between two parties, but an event consumed by fifteen teams has fifteen independent deployment schedules, fifteen on-call rotations, and fifteen backlogs. Requiring them all to deploy simultaneously with the producer is a distributed lockstep that will fail — one team is mid-incident, another is on holiday, a third has a frozen release train — and the moment the producer switches, every consumer that has not caught up breaks. So the entire strategy is about buying time: make the producer's change and the consumers' changes happen on independent schedules.

The safest technique is additive evolution. If you only ever add new optional fields and never remove, rename, or repurpose existing ones, then old consumers simply ignore the new fields and keep working, while new consumers read them. This keeps the change backward compatible, and no coordinated deploy is needed at all. Most "breaking" changes can be reframed as additive if you are willing to leave the old field in place during the transition.

```java
// Additive: add newField, keep oldField. Old consumers ignore newField and still work.
public record TransferCompleted(
    String transferId,
    long amountMinor,          // existing field — KEEP
    String currency,           // NEW optional field — old consumers ignore it
    String settlementRailV2    // NEW optional field — only new consumers read it
) {}
```

When the change genuinely cannot be additive — a field must change type or meaning — you use a new event version published alongside the old one. The producer dual-publishes: it emits both `TransferCompleted.v1` and `TransferCompleted.v2` for a controlled deprecation window. Old consumers keep reading v1; each team migrates to v2 on its own schedule; and only after every consumer has moved and you have verified v1 has no remaining readers do you stop publishing v1. A schema registry with compatibility enforcement (as with Avro or Protobuf) makes this safe by rejecting incompatible schema changes at publish time.

```java
// Dual publication during a controlled window
void publish(Transfer t) {
    bus.publish("transfer.completed.v1", toV1(t)); // old consumers
    bus.publish("transfer.completed.v2", toV2(t)); // new consumers migrate here
    // Later, after all consumers moved AND v1 has zero readers: stop publishing v1
}
```

A third technique is ordering: consumers before producers. If the new form is a superset the old consumers cannot tolerate, you first upgrade all consumers to a version that can handle both the old and new shapes, verify they are all deployed, and only then switch the producer. This is riskier than dual publication because it still requires tracking that every consumer is ready, but it avoids maintaining two event streams.

The senior-level nuance, and the heart of the question, is that the technical schema is only part of the problem. Once fifteen teams depend on your event, the event is a contract, and changing a contract with fifteen counterparties is an organizational migration. You need to discover who actually consumes the event — often there are undocumented consumers — publish a deprecation timeline, communicate it clearly, provide the new schema and migration guidance, track each consumer's migration status, and only retire the old version when tracking confirms zero usage. The compatibility technique buys you the time; the organizational process is what actually gets fifteen teams across the line. A staff-plus engineer treats the schema registry, the consumer inventory, the deprecation window, and the communication plan as equal parts of the design, because the failure mode is almost never "the schema was wrong" and almost always "we broke a consumer we did not know existed" or "a team did not migrate in time."

